# Current State

CloudFirst Banking Platform serves 1.2M users at a peak transaction volume
of 50K TPS, under a 99.9% availability SLA and a PCI DSS Level 1 compliance
obligation. The architecture in production today is six years old and last
saw a major update three years ago: a monolithic application core sitting
behind a conventional load-balancer-and-web-server tier, backed by a legacy
relational database, a document store, and a cache layer. External APIs and
batch processors feed into the same monolithic core, and a reporting system
reads directly from the legacy database.

<<mermaid>>
  flowchart TD
      A[Load Balancer] --> B[Web Servers]
      B --> C[Application Servers]
      C --> D[Monolithic Core]
      D --> E[(Legacy Database)]
      D --> F[(Document Store)]
      D --> G[(Cache Layer)]
      H[External APIs] --> C
      I[Batch Processors] --> D
      J[Reporting System] --> E

::: danger
**Critical issues identified in this review:** a monolithic architecture
that limits horizontal scaling, a legacy database creating performance
bottlenecks at current transaction volume, security gaps in authentication
and data encryption, technical debt consuming 65% of development cycles,
and operational overhead that requires 24/7 manual monitoring.
:::

This report recommends a comprehensive modernization within 14 months. The
sections below detail the findings behind that recommendation, the target
architecture we propose, and the tradeoffs the platform team weighed in
reaching it.

# Findings

## Performance and Scalability

Every layer of the current stack is running measurably hotter than its
target, and the database and storage tiers are already at critical
bottleneck levels rather than merely elevated ones. API response time
averages 350ms against a 100ms target; complex database queries take 2.5
seconds against a 500ms target; and storage I/O sits at 950 IOPS against a
2,000+ IOPS target. The overall performance rating from this assessment is
4.2 out of 10.

| Component | Current Performance | Target Performance | Bottleneck Level |
|--------------------------|-------------------------|---------------------|-------------------|
| API Response Time | 350ms average | <100ms | High |
| Database Queries | 2.5s complex queries | <500ms | Critical |
| Transaction Processing | 25K TPS peak | 50K TPS | High |
| Memory Utilization | 85% average | <70% | Medium |
| CPU Usage | 78% peak | <60% | Medium |
| Storage I/O | 950 IOPS | 2000+ IOPS | Critical |

The scalability picture is more urgent than the raw performance numbers
suggest. Projecting current growth forward, the platform is expected to
exceed its usable capacity within six months, well before any modernization
program — even an accelerated one — could complete.

<<chart: combo>>
  data:
    labels: ["Current Load", "Peak Load", "6 Month Projection", "12 Month Projection", "18 Month Projection"]
