## Security Posture

The platform relies on single-factor authentication for internal systems,
encrypts data only in transit (not at rest), applies coarse-grained access
control, and has insufficient audit logging for its PCI DSS Level 1
obligation. Measured against that standard, the platform is currently 68%
compliant against a 73% threshold — a gap the team rates as **high risk**
and recommends closing immediately, independent of the broader
modernization timeline.

## Technology Stack

Several components of the current stack are past or near end of support,
which compounds both the security and performance findings above: an
unsupported database cannot receive the security patches a PCI DSS auditor
will look for, and an unmaintained monitoring setup is part of why the
platform currently requires manual, round-the-clock observation. The
overall stack health score is 3.5 out of 10.

| Technology Category | Current Stack | Age | Support Status | Recommended Action |
|-----------------------|-----------------|---------|--------------------|----------------------------|
| Runtime Platform | Java 8 | 7 years | Extended support | Upgrade to Java 17 |
| Application Framework | Spring Boot 2.1 | 4 years | Maintenance mode | Upgrade to 3.x |
| Database | Oracle 11g | 8 years | End of life | Migrate to PostgreSQL |
| Message Queue | ActiveMQ 5.x | 6 years | Legacy | Upgrade to Kafka |
| Cache Layer | EhCache | 5 years | Limited scalability | Migrate to Redis |
| Monitoring | Custom scripts | 6 years | Unmaintained | Implement Prometheus |

# Recommendations

## Target Architecture

We recommend replacing the monolithic core with a set of twelve
domain-specific microservices — authentication, account, transaction,
payment, and notification services among them — communicating through an
event bus rather than direct synchronous calls wherever the domain allows
it. A service mesh handles the cross-cutting concerns (retries, mutual TLS,
observability) so that individual services stay focused on business logic.
Each service owns its own database, which is what makes independent
scaling and independent deployment possible in the first place.

<<mermaid>>
  flowchart TD
      A[API Gateway] --> B[Authentication Service]
      A --> C[Account Service]
      A --> D[Transaction Service]
      A --> E[Payment Service]
      A --> F[Notification Service]
      B --> G[(User Database)]
      C --> H[(Account Database)]
      D --> I[(Transaction Database)]
      E --> J[(Payment Database)]
      K[Event Bus] --> C
      K --> D
      K --> E
      K --> F
      L[Service Mesh] --> A
      L --> B
      L --> C
      L --> D
      L --> E
      L --> F

## Migration Strategy

Rather than a single cutover, we recommend a strangler-fig migration in
five phases, moving the lowest-risk services first to validate the
approach before the higher-risk transaction and payment services move.

| Phase | Duration | Services Migrated | Risk Level | Investment |
|-------|-------------|------------------------------------|------------|------------|
| Phase 1 | Months 1-3 | Authentication, Notifications | Low | $400K |
| Phase 2 | Months 4-6 | Account Management | Medium | $600K |
| Phase 3 | Months 7-10 | Transaction Processing | High | $800K |
| Phase 4 | Months 11-12 | Payment Services | High | $600K |
| Phase 5 | Months 13-14 | Legacy Decommission | Medium | $400K |

Total modernization budget is $2.8M over 14 months. The two highest-risk
phases — transaction processing and payment services — are deliberately
scheduled after authentication and account management have already proven
the migration pattern in production.

## Security Enhancement

We recommend moving to a zero-trust model across every security layer
rather than patching the current perimeter-based approach in place, since
several of the current gaps (flat network architecture, coarse-grained
access control) are structural rather than configuration issues.

| Security Layer | Current State | Target State | Implementation |
|--------------------|--------------------|------------------------|------------------------------|
| Identity & Access | Basic RBAC | Zero-trust IAM | AWS Cognito + SAML |
| Network Security | Perimeter-based | Micro-segmentation | Service mesh + VPC |
| Data Protection | Transit encryption | End-to-end encryption | KMS + field-level encryption |
| API Security | Basic validation | OAuth 2.0 + rate limiting | API Gateway + JWT |
| Monitoring | Log aggregation | SIEM + threat detection | CloudTrail + GuardDuty |
| Compliance | Manual audits | Automated compliance | Config Rules + Audit |

We recommend allocating 25% of the modernization budget to this workstream
specifically, reflecting both the current 68%-of-73% compliance gap and the
cost of a compliance failure at PCI DSS Level 1.

# Option Comparison

The platform team evaluated several infrastructure and vendor choices as
part of this review. The comparison below reflects the recommended
direction in each row, together with the alternative that was seriously
considered and why it was set aside.

| Decision Area | Recommended Option | Alternative Considered | Why the Alternative Was Set Aside |
|---------------------|-----------------------------|------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|
| Cloud Platform | AWS (Amazon EKS) | Stay on-premises | Cannot meet the 6-month scalability deadline without managed elasticity |
| Database | PostgreSQL on RDS | Stay on Oracle 11g | Oracle 11g is end-of-life; licensing cost also exceeds managed PostgreSQL |
| Message Queue | Amazon MSK (Kafka) | Upgrade ActiveMQ in place | ActiveMQ's throughput ceiling does not clear the 50K+ TPS target |
| Container Orchestration | Amazon EKS | Self-managed Kubernetes | Self-managed control plane adds operational load the team cannot absorb during migration |
| CI/CD | GitHub Actions + ArgoCD (GitOps) | Keep Jenkins | Jenkins requires more custom maintenance and lacks native GitOps rollback |
| Monitoring | Datadog + Prometheus/Grafana | Continue custom scripts | Current scripts are unmaintained and were flagged as a security gap themselves |

Taken together, these choices consolidate the platform's technology vendor
footprint from twelve primary vendors down to six, which the team expects
to reduce integration overhead as much as it reduces the direct license
cost.

## Success Metrics

| KPI | Current | Target |
|------------------------|----------|-------------------------|
| Availability | 99.9% | 99.95% |
| API response time | 350ms | <100ms (95th percentile) |
| Sustained throughput | 25K TPS | 50K TPS |
| Security compliance score | 68% | 95% |
| Deployment frequency | Weekly | Multiple daily deployments |

::: warning
The 6-month capacity horizon identified in the scalability findings above
means Phase 1 of the migration (authentication and notifications) should
begin architecture and cloud-foundation work immediately; deferring the
program start date shortens the runway available before the platform is
projected to exceed its current capacity.
:::
